   {
    "id": "ca_bastille_buttress_beckey_route",
    "name": "Bastille Buttress - Beckey Route",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.10d",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "The approach heads toward the Inyo Creek drainage below the buttress, then picks up a climbers' trail and climbs steep, loose scree slopes to the base of the route. The first pitch starts a little right of the lowest point of the buttress, up a crack system.",
    "descent": "The route tops out on a ledge system on the back of the buttress. Scramble the ledges up and left on the backside, cross the crest onto the east side, and scramble down the long gully of scree that runs beside the buttress on the left; there are othe"
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_bastille_buttress_papillon",
    "name": "Bastille Buttress - Papillon",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.11",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "The approach is the same as for the neighbouring Beckey Route: head toward the Inyo Creek drainage, pick up a climbers' trail and climb steep loose scree slopes to the base of the buttress.",
    "descent": "The route joins the Beckey line near the top and finishes at the same ledge system on the back of the buttress. Scramble the ledges up and left on the backside, cross the crest to the east side and descend the long scree gully beside the buttress on "
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_direct_south_face",
    "name": "Direct South Face",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.7",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "Park on the Tuttle Creek side, reached by the dirt road off Granite View Drive south of Whitney Portal Road, and walk the trail up the drainage to the old Stone House ruin, about a mile. Above the Stone House, follow the use trail just below it up the valley, staying high until the moraine steepens. Cairns lead through the boulder field to a point level with the start of the climb, and from there ",
    "descent": "From the plateau at the top, head east toward the large knob, pass it on its left side and aim for the sharp east ridge. Keep the ridge on your left and follow the broad gully down toward Tuttle Creek, staying left at junctions; the ground is sand an"
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_lone_pine_peak_standard_route",
    "name": "Standard route",
    "discipline": "scrambling",
    "grade": "Class 2",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "The standard route starts from the Meysan Lake trailhead inside the Whitney Portal campground: walk the campground road, cross Lone Pine Creek on the wooden bridge, pass through the summer cabin area and find the foot-trail sign for the Meysan Lakes trail. The trail climbs steadily through pine and fir. At Grass Lake, leave the trail and skirt the lake on its west side, then climb the wide chute t",
    "descent": "Return across the summit plateau to the head of the wide chute you came up, a west-facing scree gully that is easy to confuse with its neighbours, so note it on the way up. The gully is steep scree and talus, and early-season snow can lie in the uppe"
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_lone_pine_peak_summer_ridge_route",
    "name": "Summer Ridge Route",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.9",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "",
    "descent": ""
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_michael_strassman_memorial_route",
    "name": "Michael Strassman Memorial Route",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.10d",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "Park on the Tuttle Creek side, reached by the dirt road off Granite View Drive south of Whitney Portal Road, and walk the trail up the drainage to the old Stone House ruin, about a mile. From the Stone House's north doorway, follow the path down about fifty feet and watch for a left turn up the canyon, marked by cairns and switchbacks. Stay high on the sandy slopes, pass through a stand of pines a",
    "descent": "From the top of the fifth pitch or the bolted station at the bush, the choices are to rappel the route with two 60 m ropes to the notch, or to continue to the summit. To continue, follow the summer ridge up and left, or go from the top of the fifth p"
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_ne_ridge_full",
    "name": "NE Ridge (full)",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.6",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "The ridge is reached from Olivas Ranch Road on the edge of Lone Pine. From the end of that road, take the faint trail to the stream, cross it, and climb the right-hand skyline ridge on sandy class 2 ground until the rock steps begin. A nicer parking area exists at the base of the route, off the Lone Pine campground road. The full ridge is long and has no water on it, so many parties take more than",
    "descent": "Two descents are used. The east ledges descent goes down the sandy class 2 slopes with the east ridgeline in view, avoiding the steep cliffs to the south and southeast by contouring left whenever they feel close. Expect no trail or cairns, a ridge th"
   },
   {
    "id": "ca_north_ridge_4",
    "name": "North Ridge",
    "discipline": "alpine",
    "grade": "5.6",
    "description": "",
    "approach": "The approach starts at the Meysan Lake trailhead at the south end of the Whitney Portal campground, where the Meysan Lakes trail climbs steadily in switchbacks. Lone Pine Peak comes into view to the southeast. Leave the trail well below the lakes, cross the slabs toward Meysan Creek, then follow moraine to a break in the northwest face. A use trail and easy class 2 terrain angle up and left to the",
    "descent": "From the summit, hike southwest down to the plateau and cross it until you find a west-facing scree gully; several gullies look alike from above, and picking the wrong one is the usual mistake, so check the line from the valley beforehand. Once you a"
   }
